 {id:'oq1',paper:1,topic:'Applied anatomy and physiology',subtopic:'Cardiovascular response',ao:'AO2',difficulty:2,scenario:'Your midfielder is entering the final fifteen minutes of a high-intensity match.',prompt:'Explain why heart rate increases during exercise and how this helps the performer.',markPoints:[{id:'hr1',label:'Heart pumps more blood',alternatives:['pumps more blood','heart pumps faster','increases blood flow','greater cardiac output'],weight:1},{id:'hr2',label:'More oxygen reaches working muscles',alternatives:['more oxygen to working muscles','oxygen to muscles','oxygen reaches the muscles','supplies muscles with oxygen'],weight:2},{id:'hr3',label:'Carbon dioxide is removed',alternatives:['remove carbon dioxide','removes co2','take waste products away'],weight:1},{id:'hr4',label:'Supports aerobic energy release',alternatives:['aerobic energy','aerobic respiration','release more energy'],weight:1}],misconceptions:[{label:'Heart rate falls during exercise',alternatives:['heart rate decreases','heart beats slower'],penalty:20}],modelAnswer:'Heart rate rises to increase blood flow, delivering more oxygen to the working muscles and removing carbon dioxide. This supports aerobic energy release.',explanation:'The cardiovascular system responds to the greater demand for oxygen and energy in working muscles.'},
 {id:'oq2',paper:1,topic:'Physical training',subtopic:'Progressive overload',ao:'AO2',difficulty:2,scenario:'Your sprinter has completed the same programme for six weeks and performance has plateaued.',prompt:'Explain how the coach should use progressive overload to restart improvement.',markPoints:[{id:'po1',label:'Increase training demand gradually',alternatives:['increase demand gradually','gradually make training harder','progressively increase training'],weight:2},{id:'po2',label:'Change frequency, intensity, time or type',alternatives:['frequency intensity time type','increase intensity','increase frequency','increase duration','change type'],weight:2},{id:'po3',label:'Allow adaptation before increasing again',alternatives:['allow the body to adapt','after adaptation','recover and adapt'],weight:1}],misconceptions:[{label:'Large sudden increases are unsafe',alternatives:['increase everything immediately','double training straight away','maximum every day'],penalty:20}],modelAnswer:'The coach should gradually increase one FITT variable, such as intensity or training time, after the athlete has adapted to the current load.',explanation:'Progressive overload means gradually increasing training demand so that adaptation continues without an excessive rise in injury risk.'},
 {id:'oq3',paper:2,topic:'Sports psychology',subtopic:'Arousal and anxiety',ao:'AO3',difficulty:2,scenario:'Your badminton player is over-aroused before a deciding game and is rushing shots.',prompt:'Recommend a psychological technique and explain how it could improve performance.',markPoints:[{id:'ar1',label:'Identifies a suitable technique',alternatives:['deep breathing','controlled breathing','mental rehearsal','positive self talk','relaxation technique'],weight:1},{id:'ar2',label:'Reduces excessive arousal or anxiety',alternatives:['reduce arousal','lower anxiety','calm the performer','feel less nervous'],weight:2},{id:'ar3',label:'Improves focus or decision making',alternatives:['improve focus','better concentration','make better decisions','stop rushing'],weight:1},{id:'ar4',label:'Links to more controlled performance',alternatives:['more controlled shots','improve accuracy','perform the skill correctly'],weight:1}],misconceptions:[{label:'Increasing arousal further is inappropriate',alternatives:['increase arousal','make them more excited','use caffeine'],penalty:15}],modelAnswer:'Controlled breathing would reduce excessive arousal and anxiety, helping the player focus, make better decisions and execute shots with greater control.',explanation:'When arousal is too high, a relaxation technique can return the performer towards their optimal level.'},
 {id:'oq4',paper:2,topic:'Health, fitness and wellbeing',subtopic:'Recovery nutrition',ao:'AO2',difficulty:2,scenario:'Your rugby player has completed a demanding match and must recover for another fixture.',prompt:'Explain what the athlete should consume after the match and why.',markPoints:[{id:'nu1',label:'Carbohydrate replenishes glycogen',alternatives:['carbohydrate replenishes glycogen','carbs restore energy stores','replace glycogen'],weight:2},{id:'nu2',label:'Protein supports repair',alternatives:['protein repairs muscle','protein for growth and repair','repair muscle tissue'],weight:2},{id:'nu3',label:'Fluid replaces water lost in sweat',alternatives:['replace lost fluids','rehydrate','water lost through sweat'],weight:1}],misconceptions:[{label:'Avoiding fluid delays recovery',alternatives:['avoid water','do not drink','no fluids'],penalty:20}],modelAnswer:'The player should consume carbohydrate to replenish glycogen, protein to repair muscle tissue and fluid to replace water lost through sweat.',explanation:'Effective recovery requires refuelling, tissue repair and rehydration.'},
 {id:'oq5',paper:1,topic:'Physical training',subtopic:'Training methods',ao:'AO3',difficulty:3,scenario:'A football midfielder needs to repeatedly change pace throughout a full match.',prompt:'Justify why fartlek training is appropriate for this performer.',markPoints:[{id:'fa1',label:'Training contains varied intensities',alternatives:['varied intensity','changes pace','different speeds'],weight:2},{id:'fa2',label:'It mirrors the demands of football',alternatives:['mirrors football','similar to a match','sport specific'],weight:2},{id:'fa3',label:'Develops cardiovascular endurance',alternatives:['cardiovascular endurance','aerobic fitness','stamina'],weight:1},{id:'fa4',label:'Can include different terrain',alternatives:['different terrain','varied terrain','hills and flat'],weight:1}],modelAnswer:'Fartlek varies pace and can vary terrain, mirroring football’s alternating work intensities while developing cardiovascular endurance.',explanation:'The method is effective because its changing intensities are specific to the intermittent demands of football.'},
 {id:'oq6',paper:1,topic:'Applied anatomy and physiology',subtopic:'Muscle contractions',ao:'AO2',difficulty:3,scenario:'Your swimmer performs the pulling phase of front crawl against water resistance.',prompt:'Explain how the agonist and antagonist work together during a movement at a joint.',markPoints:[{id:'mu1',label:'Muscles work as an antagonistic pair',alternatives:['antagonistic pair','muscles work in pairs','agonist and antagonist'],weight:1},{id:'mu2',label:'The agonist contracts',alternatives:['agonist contracts','prime mover contracts','working muscle contracts'],weight:2},{id:'mu3',label:'The antagonist relaxes',alternatives:['antagonist relaxes','opposing muscle relaxes'],weight:2},{id:'mu4',label:'Roles reverse for the opposite movement',alternatives:['roles reverse','swap roles','opposite movement'],weight:1}],misconceptions:[{label:'Both muscles do not normally contract concentrically together',alternatives:['both contract concentrically','both muscles shorten together'],penalty:15}],modelAnswer:'The muscles operate as an antagonistic pair: the agonist contracts while the antagonist relaxes. Their roles reverse to produce the opposite movement.',explanation:'Antagonistic muscle action creates controlled movement at a joint.'},
 {id:'oq7',paper:1,topic:'Applied anatomy and physiology',subtopic:'Recovery from exercise',ao:'AO3',difficulty:3,scenario:'Your performer has completed repeated anaerobic efforts and must recover before the next fixture.',prompt:'Recommend suitable recovery methods and explain why they would help.',markPoints:[{id:'rec1',label:'Cool-down maintains blood flow',alternatives:['cool down maintains blood flow','keep heart rate elevated','active cool down'],weight:2},{id:'rec2',label:'Helps remove lactic acid or waste products',alternatives:['remove lactic acid','remove waste products','clear carbon dioxide'],weight:2},{id:'rec3',label:'Rehydrate and replace carbohydrate',alternatives:['rehydrate','replace fluids','carbohydrate for energy','restore glycogen'],weight:1},{id:'rec4',label:'Ice bath or massage may help prevent DOMS',alternatives:['ice bath','sports massage','prevent doms','delayed onset muscle soreness'],weight:1}],modelAnswer:'The performer should complete a cool-down to maintain blood flow and help remove waste products, then rehydrate and consume carbohydrate. An ice bath or massage may also be used to help prevent DOMS.',explanation:'AQA recovery content includes cool-down, diet and rehydration, ice baths and massage.'},
 {id:'oq8',paper:1,topic:'Applied anatomy and physiology',subtopic:'Antagonistic muscle action',ao:'AO2',difficulty:2,scenario:'A footballer extends the knee to strike the ball.',prompt:'Explain how the quadriceps and hamstrings work together during this action.',markPoints:[{id:'km1',label:'Quadriceps act as the agonist',alternatives:['quadriceps agonist','quadriceps contract','quadriceps are the prime mover'],weight:2},{id:'km2',label:'Hamstrings act as the antagonist',alternatives:['hamstrings antagonist','hamstrings relax','hamstring group relaxes'],weight:2},{id:'km3',label:'The knee extends',alternatives:['knee extension','extends the knee','leg straightens'],weight:1}],modelAnswer:'The quadriceps are the agonist and contract to extend the knee, while the hamstrings act as the antagonist and relax.',explanation:'Antagonistic muscle pairs create controlled movement at a joint.'},
 {id:'oq9',paper:1,topic:'Applied anatomy and physiology',subtopic:'Cardiac output',ao:'AO2',difficulty:2,scenario:'A midfielder begins a high-intensity period of play.',prompt:'Explain how cardiac output changes and why this supports performance.',markPoints:[{id:'co1',label:'Cardiac output increases',alternatives:['cardiac output increases','more blood pumped per minute'],weight:1},{id:'co2',label:'Heart rate or stroke volume increases',alternatives:['heart rate increases','stroke volume increases','heart rate and stroke volume'],weight:2},{id:'co3',label:'More oxygen reaches working muscles',alternatives:['more oxygen to muscles','oxygenated blood to working muscles'],weight:2}],modelAnswer:'Cardiac output increases because heart rate and stroke volume rise, so more oxygenated blood reaches the working muscles.',explanation:'Cardiac output equals heart rate multiplied by stroke volume.'}
